const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do you make beef jerky in an air fryer?', answer: 'To make beef jerky in an air fryer: (1) Slice lean beef 1/4 inch thin. (2) Marinate 8–24 hours. (3) Pat strips completely dry. (4) Lay strips in a single layer in the air fryer basket — do not stack. (5) Set to 160°F (dehydrator mode if available) or the lowest temperature. (6) Cook 2–4 hours, flipping every 45–60 minutes. (7) Check with the bend test.' },
  { question: 'What temperature for beef jerky in an air fryer?', answer: 'Set your air fryer to 160°F for beef jerky if it has a dehydrator mode. If your air fryer only goes to 170°F as the minimum, use that — just check at the 2-hour mark instead of 2.5 hours. Never go above 200°F in an air fryer for jerky, as that cooks the beef rather than dehydrating it.' },
  { question: 'How long does beef jerky take in an air fryer?', answer: 'Air fryer beef jerky takes 2–4 hours at 160°F, making it the fastest method for making jerky at home. The powerful airflow in air fryers accelerates moisture evaporation compared to a standard dehydrator. Thinner slices (1/8 inch) may be done in as little as 1.5 hours. Check every 45–60 minutes after the 1.5-hour mark.' },
  { question: 'Air fryer vs dehydrator for beef jerky — which is better?', answer: 'Dehydrators are better for large batches and consistency. Air fryers are better for speed and small batches (1–2 servings worth). Air fryers typically fit 4–6 oz of beef in one layer per batch, while a dehydrator holds 2+ lbs. If you make jerky regularly, a dehydrator is the better long-term investment.' },
  { question: 'How do you make beef jerky in a Ninja air fryer with dehydrator?', answer: 'Ninja DualZone and Ninja Foodi models with a dehydrator function work great for jerky. Use the Dehydrate function at 160°F for 3–4 hours. The Ninja\'s multiple rack positions allow you to fit more strips than a standard basket. Use the dehydrator trays that came with the unit — strips need airflow underneath.' },
